{"id":"h007","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Calculate the de Broglie wavelength for (a) an electron with a kinetic energy of $100 \\mathrm{eV}$\nIs the correct answer \"0.080nm\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct de Broglie wavelength for a 100 eV electron is about 0.123 nm, not 0.080 nm.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"For a non-relativistic electron the wavelength is λ = h/√(2mE).","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"100 eV is tiny compared with the electron rest energy of 511 keV, so the non-relativistic formula is accurate.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"λ ≈ 1.226 nm/√(E in eV) gives 0.1226 nm for 100 eV.","confidence":0.96,"lean":-1,"theta":1.508,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Relativistic corrections are on the order of 0.01% and do not change the result.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A wavelength of 0.080 nm would require an energy of about 235 eV rather than 100 eV.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The 0.080 nm value likely comes from a calculation error or a different energy.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.0996,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Direct substitution into λ = h/√(2mE) clearly gives about 0.123 nm, which decides the question.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.4,"rela